  "name": "Question 20",
  "problem": "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{2x-{x}^{4}}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the limit as\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{2x-{x}^{4}}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{{x}^{4}-2x}.\n$$\n\nThe numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|-5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x|\\le 5.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator ${x}^{4}-2x$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{2x-{x}^{4}}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{{x}^{4}-2x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{2x-{x}^{4}},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951126/assets/0865e078246952abcfcd570887a38edfb8120066cd6ee9ad54edc61bac5ddc52.png)",
